[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138297 :question/id #uuid "15bbccd7-d0ce-50bb-80bb-777b5d0a7245" :question/math-academy-id "e-4360" :question/problem "Factor $x^4-4x^2+4.$" :question/worked-solution "We can introduce a new variable $z=x^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+4 & = \\\\ {({x}^{2})}^{2}-4({x}^{2})+4 & = \\\\ {z}^{2}-4z+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic as the square of a difference, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-4z+4 & = \\\\ (z-2)^{2} & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138302 :question/id #uuid "da0223c6-ff03-55da-992c-d8a1ef3f9aff" :question/math-academy-id "e-4999" :question/problem "Factor the expression $x^3 - 6x^2 + 9x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 6x^2 + 9x = x(x^2 - 6x + 9)\n$$\n\nThe expression $x^2 - 6x + 9$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}-6x+9) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}-6x+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ x(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138303 :question/id #uuid "007b9b7b-5fab-5e50-9598-fc94a9f6edde" :question/math-academy-id "e-5000" :question/problem "Factor the expression $x^3 + x^2 - 2x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x & =x({x}^{2}+x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial $x^2 + x - 2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}+x-2) & = \\\\ x(({x}^{2}-x)+(2x-2)) & = \\\\ x(x(x-1)+2(x-1)) & = \\\\ x(x+2)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138304 :question/id #uuid "12f9f52c-0baa-557b-83b7-d80c8a34c409" :question/math-academy-id "e-5018" :question/problem "Factor $t^4 - t^2 - 2.$" :question/worked-solution "We can introduce a new variable $z = t^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-2 & = \\\\ {({t}^{2})}^{2}-{t}^{2}-2 & = \\\\ {z}^{2}-z-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-z-2 & = \\\\ (z+1)(z-2) & = \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138305 :question/id #uuid "f503abb5-10a5-56bf-b07b-7ebe1ed04049" :question/math-academy-id "e-5019" :question/problem "Factor $2p^4 + 5p^2 + 3.$" :question/worked-solution "We can introduce a new variable $t = p^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{p}^{4}+5{p}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ 2{({p}^{2})}^{2}+5{p}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+5t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+5t+3 & = \\\\ (t+1)(2t+3) & = \\\\ ({p}^{2}+1)(2{p}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138314 :question/id #uuid "638af342-1995-5403-84bd-b6c720cb432d" :question/math-academy-id "e-907" :question/problem "A guitar string, made of carbon steel, has a linear mass density of $3\\,950$ milligrams per meter. What is the linear mass density in grams per meter?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{mg}$ with $\\textrm{g}.$ To get rid of $\\textrm{mg},$ we can put $\\textrm{mg}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\textrm{g},$ we can put $\\textrm{g}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{mg}} }{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{\\cancel{ \\textrm{mg} } } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg} }{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{ \\textrm{mg} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The density given in the problem statement is $3\\,950 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ milligrams per gram. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}$ grams per milligram.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,950\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{m}} & =3\\,950\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mg}} \\\\  & =(3\\,950\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000})\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mg}}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mg}}} \\\\  & =3.95\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{m}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the linear mass density of the string is $3.95\\,\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{m}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138315 :question/id #uuid "08aa3547-cb51-5d00-8cb7-3e1a9bd4a695" :question/math-academy-id "e-908" :question/problem "Sand runs through a sand timer at a rate of $120$ grams per minute. What is this rate in grams per second?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{min}$ with $\\textrm{s}.$ To get rid of $\\textrm{min},$ we can put $\\textrm{min}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\textrm{s},$ we can put $\\textrm{s}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{ \\cancel{\\textrm{min}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{ \\cancel{ \\textrm{min} } }{ \\textrm{s} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{ \\textrm{min}}\\cdot \\dfrac{  \\textrm{min}  }{ \\textrm{s} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $120 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ seconds per minute. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ minutes per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}} & =120\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =(120\\cdot \\frac{1}{60})\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the sand runs through the sand timer at a rate of $2 \\,\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1339f146-a08c-5ad9-91a3-9b147b1bc670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458655} {:answer/id #uuid "65388c14-2a1f-52cd-976a-bed1c888cdba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+3y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458807} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7d7a53-55e8-5f2f-9c10-6d5a424edbf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458821} {:answer/id #uuid "ae535526-7d85-5ba9-b807-a102b34f9fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458937} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3e569e-56d3-51b6-8d55-b645d14d4c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+3y-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1339f146-a08c-5ad9-91a3-9b147b1bc670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458655} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d7a36e8-2de2-55b3-b550-60c5776091ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4b0c07a2-e8d1-5e15-bed8-9c8ae4b42423" :question/math-academy-id "q-2307" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{3}(3x-9y+27)=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the distributive law and simplifying, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(3x-9y+27) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3x-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9y+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 27 & = \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3}-\\frac{9y}{3}+\\frac{27}{3} & = \\\\ x-3y+9\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c82486-3253-577c-bede-0a02c498f9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{3}-b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458635} {:answer/id #uuid "3568da3f-8e6a-5e66-a04c-51f138005543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a-\\frac{3b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458733} {:answer/id #uuid "46360383-ff00-536a-83ae-8d05da42d7bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a-b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458755} {:answer/id #uuid "5f117f77-e934-5833-bbf8-d08521e2502b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{3}-\\frac{3b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458789} {:answer/id #uuid "eee94a81-f3dc-5550-b98b-500945c6fd9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a+\\frac{3b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3568da3f-8e6a-5e66-a04c-51f138005543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a-\\frac{3b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458733} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9cf5602-d832-5032-92f6-e49d0c641515" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ddb24f3-09aa-59e7-9969-7879cb03c6cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-43919" :question/problem "$3(\\frac{a}{3}-\\frac{b}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the distributive law and simplifying, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(\\frac{a}{3}-\\frac{b}{2}) & = \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{a}{3}+3\\cdot (-\\frac{b}{2}) & = \\\\ \\frac{3a}{3}+(-\\frac{3b}{2}) & = \\\\ a-\\frac{3b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b42599b7-aa2d-5b6b-a16a-905840183e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{4}-4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483119} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4d5186-31f2-598a-8731-77d8fc7ead72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483126} {:answer/id #uuid "c64f0b40-8b5d-549b-b79b-83998b1f88fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483144} {:answer/id #uuid "e253494d-ebf2-5ebc-b66a-512100b15481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483195} {:answer/id #uuid "e72f770f-90f5-5981-8fd1-ae24118175e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b42599b7-aa2d-5b6b-a16a-905840183e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{4}-4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483119} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3df818c-c0f8-52cb-b321-2a2f2422fa26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186137854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cc5ea5d-34bf-5afb-9604-06d8fc57b7fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-61980" :question/problem "$(3{x}^{2}-2y)(3{x}^{2}+2y)=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)(a-b)={a}^{2}-{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3{x}^{2}$ and $b=2y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-2y)(3{x}^{2}+2y) & = \\\\ {(3{x}^{2})}^{2}-(2y)^{2} & = \\\\ 9{x}^{4}-4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1915d7ff-b586-5fb5-90f4-d69da270222f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2n}+9{y}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482860} {:answer/id #uuid "754ba023-6388-50a6-b77b-a9be36a73709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2n}-9{y}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483014} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e00615-af87-5a34-8ad3-47a40357e4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2n}-6{x}^{n}{y}^{n}-9{y}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483118} {:answer/id #uuid "d4151343-07d5-5eba-9fab-f9f55fdb3683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2n}+6{x}^{n}{y}^{n}-9{y}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483170} {:answer/id #uuid "e3950d0a-a3f2-5ea2-abeb-da47c233ce45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2n}+3{x}^{n}{y}^{n}-9{y}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "754ba023-6388-50a6-b77b-a9be36a73709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2n}-9{y}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483014} :answer-field/id #uuid "a11c2dbd-e448-5acd-a9fd-135dd7b173da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186137860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ba1b73e-76fe-564b-a95b-5d83b106f98e" :question/math-academy-id "q-61981" :question/problem "$({x}^{n}+3{y}^{n})({x}^{n}-3{y}^{n})=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)(a-b)={a}^{2}-{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a={x}^{n}$ and $b=3{y}^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{n}+3{y}^{n})({x}^{n}-3{y}^{n}) & = \\\\ {({x}^{n})}^{2}-(3{y}^{n})^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2n}-{3}^{2}\\cdot ({y}^{n})^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2n}-9{y}^{2n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e67e45d-eca1-5e1d-ab05-8a2c8d157c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(3x-1)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529725} {:answer/id #uuid "47926ffc-d7a1-5797-b730-73328c2c7beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(3x+1)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529747} {:answer/id #uuid "73678dff-3f54-569b-89c0-a6eceb3259bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-1)^{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529873} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7f324c-5df5-5864-a6e0-aba438969f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-1)(x+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529924} {:answer/id #uuid "df8b8d89-7aa9-54b3-a94a-df6ecca6f6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(3x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e67e45d-eca1-5e1d-ab05-8a2c8d157c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(3x-1)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529725} :answer-field/id #uuid "81a252b4-5456-5662-8e25-2711dd297043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186137944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e190f45b-204a-5b29-a95b-506ea9c23984" :question/math-academy-id "q-69249" :question/problem "Factor the expression $3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x & =x(3{x}^{2}+2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial $3{x}^{2}+2x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3{x}^{2}+2x-1) & = \\\\ x((3{x}^{2}+3x)+(-x-1)) & = \\\\ x(3x(x+1)-1(x+1)) & = \\\\ x(3x-1)(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142493 :question/id #uuid "46fd744d-5c52-5866-821d-7acf7f39f6b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10722" :question/problem "A manufacturer of bias tape must package $9,252$ yards of tape into rolls containing $5$ yards each. After packaging, how many yards of tape will be left over?" :question/worked-solution "To determine how many yards of the tape will be left, we need to find the remainder of $9,252\\div 5$.\n\nTo divide $9,252$ by $5$, we use long division:\n\n| $1$$8$$5$$0$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$2$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$5$$0$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$2$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n9,252 \\div 5 = 1,850 \\,\\text{R}\\, {\\color{blue}{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, $1,850$ rolls of tape will be packaged into rolls, and $\\color{blue}2$ yards will be left over."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142494 :question/id #uuid "667094b8-4cb0-59e4-92f9-f7562439af2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10723" :question/problem "Deborah, a clothing company employee, has to pack $112$ shirts into boxes. If each box can hold up to $6$ shirts, what is the smallest number of boxes that she needs to pack the shirts?" :question/worked-solution "To figure out the smallest number of boxes Deborah needs, we need to divide $112\\div 6$.\n\nTo divide $112$ by $6$, we use long division:\n\n|  |  | $1$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$ |  |  |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $6$ |  |\n|  |  | $5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n|  |  |  | $4$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n112 \\div 6 = 18 \\,\\text{R}\\, {\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nThis means that Deborah will fill $18$ boxes and have ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ shirts left over.\n\nTo hold the leftover shirts, Deborah will need an additional box. Therefore, she will need\n\n$$\n18+1 =19\n$$\n\nboxes in total."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142495 :question/id #uuid "2601ad66-1d73-5c2f-8be7-4184ef4c3d33" :question/math-academy-id "e-10735" :question/problem "An architect orders $563$ boards for a project. If the boards they need come in packs of $4$, how many fully-filled packs will they receive?" :question/worked-solution "To find out the number of fully-filled packs the architect will receive, we need to compute the quotient of $563 \\div 4$.\n\nTo divide $563$ by $4$, we use long division:\n\n| $1$$4$$0$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$6$$0$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$0$${3}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n563 \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{140}}\\,\\text{R}\\,3\n$$\n\nSo, the architect will receive ${\\color{blue}{140}}$ fully-filled packs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142595 :question/id #uuid "d2914b4d-bbb5-5433-b5bb-312caff3e2c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13714" :question/problem "Calculate $12 + 30 \\div (6 + 9)$." :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 + 30 \\div (6 + 9) &=  \\\\\n&= 12 + 30 \\div 15 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 + 30 \\div 15 &=  \\\\\n&= 12 + 2 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add and subtract, from left to right:\n\n$$\n12 + 2 =14\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142596 :question/id #uuid "947ef370-3cdf-5566-b573-b0c74d2b1424" :question/math-academy-id "e-13716" :question/problem "Evaluate $[10 - (1 + 3)] \\div 2$." :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the inner parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[10 - (1 + 3)] \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n&= [10 - 4] \\div 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[10 - 4] \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n&= 6 \\div 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142628 :question/id #uuid "8848f5c1-767c-5cd3-815d-89a120e4eb03" :question/math-academy-id "e-13856" :question/problem "Evaluate $6+8-12$." :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations.\n\nIn this case, we add and subtract from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 + 8 - 12 &=  \\\\\n14 - 12 &=  \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142629 :question/id #uuid "f1553aa8-81d4-5e53-8307-c64264d8a3ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-13858" :question/problem "Calculate $12 \\div 6 \\times 4$." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the given expression, we follow the order of operations.\n\nIn this case, we multiply and divide from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 \\div 6 \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n12 \\div 6 \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142630 :question/id #uuid "dc443bef-be76-5fba-bd54-6d0f678264b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13862" :question/problem "Evaluate $3 + 2 \\times 4$." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + 2 \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n3 + 2 \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n&= 3 + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + 8 &= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142632 :question/id #uuid "ad541341-5c04-5fce-a672-96655ce937ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-13865" :question/problem "Calculate $4 \\times 5 + 6 \\div 3$." :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations.\n\nFirst, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 \\cdot 5 + 6 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot 5 + 6 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n20 + 6 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n20 + 6 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 20 + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20 + 2 &= 22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142665 :question/id #uuid "c503bf6e-cf3b-5ba2-a2f4-08229e9098d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1831" :question/problem "A fisherman evenly distributed $61$ pounds of fish into $4$ boxes. How many pounds of fish did he put in each box?" :question/worked-solution "We need to work out $61\\div 4$. We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}5$$4$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\color{red}1$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n61\\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{15}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($4$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n61 \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}15} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fisherman put\n\n$$\n15 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\npounds of fish in each box."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142666 :question/id #uuid "d3ca57d3-fd57-573d-8e1b-ab71562c9bc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-1832" :question/problem "What is $74 \\div 6$?" :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}2$$6$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{red}2$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n74\\div 6 = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($6$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n74\\div 6 = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fractional part by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6} = \\dfrac{2 \\div 2}{6 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the simplified mixed number is\n\n$$\n74\\div 6 = 12 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142667 :question/id #uuid "061d6a29-9909-5abb-baf1-5fb250befeb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-1833" :question/problem "What is $942 \\div 9\\,$?" :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}0$$\\color{blue}4$$9$$\\!\\!\\phantom{\\mid}\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$0$$4$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$\\color{red}6$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n942 \\div 9 = {\\color{blue}{104}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{6}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($9$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n942 \\div 9 = {\\color{blue}{104}} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{9}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fractional part by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{9} = \\dfrac{6 \\div 3}{9 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the simplified mixed number is\n\n$$\n942 \\div 9 = 104 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142721 :question/id #uuid "d9824a14-6214-5753-b312-7a25e8dbcd19" :question/math-academy-id "e-2305" :question/problem "What is $9.888 \\div 24$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the problem using long division notation. We keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $24$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\: \\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$24$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $0$$\\!\\!. 4$$1$$2$$24$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$6$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$4$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n9.888 \\div 24 = 0.412\\,\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142722 :question/id #uuid "38730f23-7bf6-5e1f-9536-c26ebefded46" :question/math-academy-id "e-2306" :question/problem "A pump delivered $367.5$ liters of water in $3.5$ hours. How many liters of water would the pump deliver every hour if it worked at a steady pace?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the number of liters of water the pump would deliver in $1$ hour, we need to calculate $367.5\\div 3.5$.\n\nWe can write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{367.5}{3.5}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{367.5 \\times 10}{3.5 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{3,675}{35}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $3,675 \\div 35$.\n\nNext, we proceed to use long division:\n\n| $1$$0$$5$$35\\,$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$1$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$0$$1$$7$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$7$$5$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n367.5\\div 3.5 = 105\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the pump would deliver $105$ liters of water every hour."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142723 :question/id #uuid "5cc37af5-51d9-553e-a6a0-666b990139cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-2307" :question/problem "What is the value of $73.71 \\div 5.4$?" :question/worked-solution "We can write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{73.71}{5.4}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{73.71 \\times 10}{5.4 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{737.1}{54}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $737.1 \\div 54$.\n\nNext, we write down the problem using long division notation, where we keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $54$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 1}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\,\\! \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid \\mid}} \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$54$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 1}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $1$$3$$\\!\\!\\!. 6$$5$$54$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$1$$9$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$6$$2$$3$$5$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$2$$4$$2$$7$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n73.71 \\div 5.4 = 13.65\\,\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147180 :question/id #uuid "a7afb36a-256c-5a76-b564-841d23f8604f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14461" :question/problem "Given that the mean of the data set below is $5$, what is the mean absolute deviation of the data?\n\n$9, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\: 6$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the mean is ${\\color{blue}5}$.\n\nSo, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute values of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}9 - 5 & + & 2 - 5 & + & 3 - 5 & + & 6 - 5\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 & + & -3 & + & -2 & + & 1\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n4 + 3 + 2 + 1 &=  \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}10}$) by the total number of data points ($4$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}10}{4} = 2.5\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147181 :question/id #uuid "59f74f08-b299-503e-97c1-7a95f0287e79" :question/math-academy-id "e-14462" :question/problem "Find the mean absolute deviation of the data set below.\n$3, \\: 1, \\: 4, \\: 8$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mean of the data:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\text{mean}} = \\dfrac{3 + 1 + 4 + 8}{4} = \\dfrac{16}{4} = {\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute values of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 - 4 & + & 1 - 4 & + & 4 - 4 & + & 8 - 4\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & + & -3 & + & 0 & + & 4\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n1 + 3 + 0 + 4 &=  \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}8}$) by the total number of data points ($4$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{4} = 2\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147182 :question/id #uuid "ada2db5b-9257-5dfe-8322-5efe47c46457" :question/math-academy-id "e-14465" :question/problem "The total times of five racers during a Grand Prix were:\n$14 \\,\\text{minutes}, \\quad 16 \\,\\text{minutes}, \\quad 13 \\,\\text{minutes}, \\quad 21 \\,\\text{minutes}, \\quad 19 \\,\\text{minutes}$\nRounded to one decimal place, what is the mean absolute deviation of the times?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mean of the data:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\text{mean}} = \\dfrac{14 + 16 + 13 + 21 + 19}{5} = \\dfrac{83}{5} = {\\color{blue}16.6}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute values of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}14 - 16.6 & + & 16 - 16.6 & + & 13 - 16.6 & + & 21 - 16.6 & + & 19 - 16.6\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2.6 & + & -0.6 & + & -3.6 & + & 4.4 & + & 2.4\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n2.6 + 0.6 + 3.6 + 4.4 + 2.4 &=  \\\\\n&= 13.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}13.6}$) by the total number of data points ($5$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}13.6}{5} = 2.72\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest tenth gives:\n$2.72 \\rightarrow 2.7$\n\nTherefore, the mean absolute deviation of the times of the racers is $2.7$ minutes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147232 :question/id #uuid "babd5e8c-e5e9-5e5b-9b83-198bbccf41dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21851" :question/problem "$$\nx^2y'' + x(x-3)y' - 6y = 0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0,$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n= \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^{\\lambda+n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives $y'$ and $y''{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(x-3){y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}-3x)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y'$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(x-3)y'\n=\n\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty (\\lambda+m-1)a_{m-1}x^{\\lambda+m}\n-\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty 3(\\lambda+n)a_nx^{\\lambda+n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the ${\\color{blue}n=0}$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-3){y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-3\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-3\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the ${\\color{blue}n=0}$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -3\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\geq 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n$\\displaystyle\nx^{\\lambda}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n,$\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda),$ $n\\geq 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\neq0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147234 :question/id #uuid "46371f2c-f7f3-5302-81b8-8ab1a21cfff8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21897" :question/problem "$$\n12x^2y'' + 7xy' + (2x-3)y = 0\n$$\n\nFind the possible values of $\\lambda$ for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above, and write the corresponding Frobenius series forms.\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =12({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (2x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0,$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $\\color{blue}n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =12({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (2x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (12{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\geq 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n$\\displaystyle\nx^{\\lambda}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n,$\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda),$ $n \\geq 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\neq0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine the possible values of $\\lambda.$ Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda -3)(3\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions have the forms\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants $a_n$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147320 :question/id #uuid "0eb9dcfe-52d1-5c63-8d9d-bc90d45724cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-2808" :question/problem "The dot plot below shows the total time members of a rock band practiced their instruments last week. Each dot represents a different band member. What is the mean absolute deviation in the band members' practice times?\n\n![](images/c0/c02f5d5c0919d53727de8dced67d986e10c6fdaf3f0dc8288c0a29edfc4c54d7.png)" :question/worked-solution "The dot plot tells us the following:\n\n- $1$ member practiced for $3$ hours\n- $1$ member practiced for $4$ hours\n- $1$ member practiced for $5$ hours\n- $2$ members practiced for $6$ hours\n- $1$ member practiced for $9$ hours\n\nSo, we get the following data:\n\n$$\n3, \\: 4, \\: 5, \\: 6, \\: 6, \\: 9\n$$\n\nNow, we find the mean of the data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean &= ((3 \\cdot 1) + (4 \\cdot 1) + (5 \\cdot 1) + (6 \\cdot 2) + (9 \\cdot 1))/(6) \\\\\n&= \\frac{33}{6} \\\\\n&= 5.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 - 5.5 & + & 4 - 5.5 & + & 5 - 5.5 & + & 6 - 5.5 & + & 6 - 5.5 & + & 9 - 5.5\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2.5 & + & -1.5 & + & -0.5 & + & 0.5 & + & 0.5 & + & 3.5\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n2.5 + 1.5 + 0.5 + 0.5 + 0.5 + 3.5 &=  \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}9}$) by the total number of data points ($6$). This gives\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}9}{6} = 1.5\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a2196d-53fd-52de-b8a0-124a38e3bd89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315253} {:answer/id #uuid "2a79f319-8357-515a-9799-68d195eab679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315363} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf248e7-caf4-5999-85f8-eae1017c91bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315463} {:answer/id #uuid "6032ae92-09c8-532e-8882-576ed3105ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315658} {:answer/id #uuid "a3de4965-a412-5d66-b81f-da04f80e5209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a79f319-8357-515a-9799-68d195eab679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315363} :answer-field/id #uuid "0425ddf4-1b24-5e67-97e9-e8b046293eb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0e14434-a26e-5107-b22c-b44308228f81" :question/math-academy-id "q-342074" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x(x-2){y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x(x-2){y}^{′} & =(2{x}^{2}-4x)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -4y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the ${y}^{′}$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x(x-2){y}^{′}={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +m-1){a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(x-2){y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[2(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -4y & =-4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -4\\lambda -4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339497c9-46c0-5612-a360-ea3afdc5f5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315421} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbd52da-0ca0-56b5-81d5-60d19e9bafba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315551} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4457de-ea89-52dd-9607-6e81ca227a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315799} {:answer/id #uuid "a37d1b06-ef97-528c-a87f-a903363a1e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316040} {:answer/id #uuid "e27bf12e-971b-544d-b7e8-fe52379ef938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "339497c9-46c0-5612-a360-ea3afdc5f5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315421} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d4fd735-cd77-5e78-9493-e0fe13bb9b6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76de2b1a-200b-50ab-962d-beae9047d678" :question/math-academy-id "q-342276" :question/problem "$$\n4x{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{″} & =4x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 2{y}^{′} & =2\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $n=m-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-y=-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n-y=-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 2{y}^{′} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form ${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$ for some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ 2(2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6d16e1d-7430-549a-811c-dc9f089d8775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n + 1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144481} {:answer/id #uuid "e756f3ac-6466-5f18-935e-9f0abdc09cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n - 1/8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144482} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1d0c5b-45ab-5047-8cca-0294179bad34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n + 1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144483} {:answer/id #uuid "47775fbf-8b80-5deb-8ae2-150b52eedb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n + 3/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144484} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6097f8-af24-54af-adfb-4a2c207d8283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n - 1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144485}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cd158e97-1195-5b41-8027-139370ced5f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144480} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8098ee5-128b-5fb8-91f2-a22403961e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379655} {:answer/id #uuid "76c3489a-d6da-5d44-9bb1-6d6f133397ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379656} {:answer/id #uuid "2593e8ca-7b3f-56f1-a981-67d253be0e4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379657} {:answer/id #uuid "2c05f408-d2d5-5f3a-8e3d-292bd31abb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379658} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac0bf6e-fd51-57ff-a4af-f8b9f41ff580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ac0bf6e-fd51-57ff-a4af-f8b9f41ff580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379659} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c93baba-2564-562a-aca0-907f0cbd73a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35a47c54-bd01-5092-9737-03f17d83169c" :question/math-academy-id "q-344002" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n8{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+10x{y}^{′}+(x-1)y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 10x{y}^{′} & =10\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 10x{y}^{′} & =10\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\ (x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+10\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +10\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151820 :question/id #uuid "fc9992be-d6e0-569b-b0a9-7ff40a8c0e22" :question/math-academy-id "e-12831" :question/problem "Evaluate $-7 + 3.1 - 5.$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe add and subtract, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7+3.1-5 & = \\\\ (-7+3.1)-5 & = \\\\ -\\,3.9-5 & = \\\\ -8.9\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151821 :question/id #uuid "9d5382d3-5153-5457-ae03-26736f3a123a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12832" :question/problem "What is the value of $2 \\div 6 \\times (-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe divide and multiply, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2÷6\\times (-5) & = \\\\ (2÷6)\\times (-5) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{6}\\times (-5) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\times (-5) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{(-5)}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times (-5)}{3\\times 1} & = \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151822 :question/id #uuid "216c39cc-456d-5bf8-abbc-2860ab3ebb43" :question/math-academy-id "e-12833" :question/problem "Evaluate $-2 + 6 \\div (-10).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+6÷(-10) & = \\\\ -2+(6÷(-10)) & = \\\\ -2+(-0.6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+(-0.6) & =-2.6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151823 :question/id #uuid "1fedcc75-84fe-5f3f-9555-896493c91232" :question/math-academy-id "e-12834" :question/problem "What is the value of $\\left( -3 + 6 \\right) \\div \\dfrac{4}{3} - \\dfrac{1}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3+6)÷\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{4} & = \\\\ 3÷\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3÷\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{4} & = \\\\ (3÷\\frac{4}{3})-\\frac{1}{4} & = \\\\ (\\frac{3}{1}÷\\frac{4}{3})-\\frac{1}{4} & = \\\\ (\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{4})-\\frac{1}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\times 3}{1\\times 4}-\\frac{1}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{9-1}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{4} & = \\\\ 8÷4 & = \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152022 :question/id #uuid "6c52121e-f717-5605-9d50-8e66f7b47bf1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7483" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\phi(210)$, where $\\phi$ is Euler's totient function." :question/worked-solution "Given a natural number $n$, Euler's totient function $\\phi(n)$ returns the total number of positive integers that are less than $n$ and are coprime to $n$.\n\nRecall that Euler's totient function is a multiplicative function, meaning that if two positive integers $m$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\n\\phi(mn) = \\phi(m)\\phi(n)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n210 = 5\\cdot 6 \\cdot 7\n$$\n\n- Since $5$ and $7$ are distinct prime numbers, we have\n$ϕ(5)|= 5 - 1 = 4,; ϕ(7)|= 7 - 1 = 6$.\n- To compute $\\phi(6)$, let's write down all positive integers that are less than $6$ and cancel out the numbers that are not coprime to $6$:\n$\\require{cancel} 1, \\: \\bcancel{2}, \\: \\bcancel{3}, \\bcancel{4},\\: 5$\nSo, $\\phi(6) = 2$.\n\nFinally, since $\\phi$ is multiplicative and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(5,6,7)=1\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(210) &= ϕ(5) \\cdot ϕ(6) \\cdot ϕ(7) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166564 :question/id #uuid "d2127a8e-14bb-5574-a542-852333fe4cca" :question/math-academy-id "e-21789" :question/problem "Let $F(x) = 2x^2+5$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ to $x\\in \\mathbb R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx)\\right).\n$$\n\nDetermine the integral expression for the coefficient $a_4.$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left( a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0,$ $a_n,$ and $b_n$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\cos(4x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1, \\sin(x), \\cos(x), \\sin(2x), \\cos(2x), \\ldots$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\na_4 \\left\\langle \\cos(4x), \\cos(4x) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={a}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166613 :question/id #uuid "b2d41cac-2c65-5d2d-b847-0c1f9bfdf48a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8396" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u = \\arcosh{u}+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac {x}{2} \\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u = \\dfrac {x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $2\\sqrt{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\cdot (2 \\, \\textrm{d}u) \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\arcosh u \\Big|_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\arcosh{\\sqrt 5} - \\arcosh{2} \\[5pt]\n\n&=  \\ln\\left(\\sqrt 5 + \\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt 5\\right)^2 - 1}\\right) - \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{\\left(2\\right)^2 - 1}\\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{5}\\right) -  \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n& = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2 + \\sqrt{5}}{2 + \\sqrt{3}}  \\right)\n\n}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166635 :question/id #uuid "7af9f5f6-1e14-55ea-8a09-25d211cb40d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8435" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\ln 3}^{\\ln 6} \\dfrac{\\csch{x}}{\\coth^2{x} - 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cosecant-cotangent identity \n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n1 + \\cot^2 x= \\csc^2{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic cosecant-cotangent identity\n\n$$\n\\coth^2x - 1=\\csch^2{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int_{\\ln 3}^{\\ln 6} \\dfrac{\\csch{x}}{\\coth^2{x} - 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n\t\t\t&= \\int_{\\ln 3}^{\\ln 6} \\dfrac{\\csch{x}}{\\csch^2{x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\int_{\\ln 3}^{\\ln 6} \\sinh{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\cosh{x} \\Big|_{\\ln 3}^{\\ln 6}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\cosh(\\ln 6) - \\cosh(\\ln 3)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left(\\dfrac{e^{\\ln 6}+e^{-\\ln 6}}{2}\\right) - \\left(\\dfrac{e^{\\ln 3}+e^{-\\ln 3}}{2}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left(\\dfrac{6 + \\dfrac{1}{6}}{2}\\right) - \\left(\\dfrac{3 + \\dfrac{1}{3}}{2}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{37}{12} - \\dfrac{5}{3}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{37}{12} - \\dfrac{20}{12} \\[5pt]\n            &= \\dfrac{17}{12}\n\t}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2236311d-1dcf-5814-8621-6ca58ac136e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397400} {:answer/id #uuid "80766049-c81a-5e1