8 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397408} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd3c9f2-b034-51ed-b959-1917ee861c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397409} {:answer/id #uuid "a0938cb1-fa06-51a3-9682-a27947461754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397410} {:answer/id #uuid "16682102-e4e9-5e8e-b234-f5f85ae7a7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebd3c9f2-b034-51ed-b959-1917ee861c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397409} :answer-field/id #uuid "acd80bdd-4dbe-5452-b9e2-03bec982eb3d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397406} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6896852-8238-5500-8785-a40ba1143f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397413} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0541cc-7371-58fe-8dca-eb545f614b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397414} {:answer/id #uuid "610883b1-a3e9-5606-b9ae-cf0dd1218917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397415} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3717c9-e1f6-51ef-9d7f-0c5c74af7ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397416} {:answer/id #uuid "23137492-fa85-5ebf-bdf6-f3faffc43cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23137492-fa85-5ebf-bdf6-f3faffc43cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397417} :answer-field/id #uuid "d466e0c2-5e9a-5545-9677-019c4fb5c4a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa20d8e3-c4d9-5112-bd9c-ec30f6aaa6fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-340418" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{5}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-10\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef2209e-3d8f-5297-ad7a-edc869e85aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413355} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed25c1f-08d5-5b38-8bf8-b0a0bfd412ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413357} {:answer/id #uuid "88d440e0-29f7-5376-a757-92d36791c9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413364} {:answer/id #uuid "c052414d-62b2-5ddf-8d38-b8519a51bb8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413371} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ac8e7f-277f-5ca0-83dd-f93b1ee8e219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ed25c1f-08d5-5b38-8bf8-b0a0bfd412ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413357} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a51accb-bbae-52f7-8c5d-2c3c83aae97b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "281d8718-96cc-5786-9125-bdda8d8dfa5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413354} {:answer/id #uuid "734b3ad1-94c6-5ccc-a8b8-d77fb92327cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413360} {:answer/id #uuid "74949511-a7ef-5835-b1ed-11c9565189c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413361} {:answer/id #uuid "b4aa1927-cea8-52e9-87f5-49a49eb90e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413369} {:answer/id #uuid "f656d122-43f2-55e3-b008-141d9bf4c09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4aa1927-cea8-52e9-87f5-49a49eb90e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413369} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2554085-77fe-5157-9ee0-fb327d6f9788" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413390} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cdcf984-3000-5dc4-be61-ef3673edd14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413356} {:answer/id #uuid "7d216d93-03a2-52c7-8109-b053ea99a0b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413362} {:answer/id #uuid "c04eff61-d1a2-5922-831e-05e57e1cedbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413370} {:answer/id #uuid "de6a0d2c-a56d-5bb1-9b1c-cd4e2312cdc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413376} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd7dac4-b5f5-51c0-9495-3c31b7bdebf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨1,1⟩}{⟨f(x),1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cdcf984-3000-5dc4-be61-ef3673edd14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413356} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe88487e-f897-504c-b0b7-1ea54245c515" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5082df1a-d560-5493-a305-43900a17419a" :question/math-academy-id "q-341306" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+9x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{0}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩} \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171714 :question/id #uuid "8b27c37f-10a7-5e55-8230-916bb246142b" :question/math-academy-id "e-1238" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\left| - \\dfrac{x^2}{10} \\right|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|{-a}| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{x^2}{10} \\right|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{x}^{2}}{10}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|{x}^{2}|}{|10|} & = \\\\ \\frac{|{x}^{2}|}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x^2 \\geq 0$ for any real $x,$ we have that $\\left|x^2\\right|=x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|{x}^{2}|}{10} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171715 :question/id #uuid "e0d4030c-4490-5e36-af26-7ded6003e841" :question/math-academy-id "e-1239" :question/problem "Given that $x\\lt 0,$ simplify $|{-6x}|+2x.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|{-a}| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|6x|+2x.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|6x|+2x & = \\\\ |6|\\cdot |x|+2x & = \\\\ 6\\cdot |x|+2x & = \\\\ 6|x|+2x & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x \\lt 0,$ we have that $|x|=-x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6|x|+2x & = \\\\ 6(-x)+2x & = \\\\ -6x+2x & = \\\\ -4x & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171819 :question/id #uuid "9c4b5b23-3d35-593a-ae69-4766e5b1d996" :question/math-academy-id "e-6800" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $|{-3z}|-5\\cdot|4z|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|{-a}| = |a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|3z|-5\\cdot|4z|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3z|-5\\cdot |4z| & = \\\\ |3|\\cdot |z|-5\\cdot |4|\\cdot |z| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |z|-5\\cdot 4\\cdot |z| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |z|-20\\cdot |z| & = \\\\ 3|z|-20|z| & = \\\\ -17|z| & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1aa2fee-c2fb-55e9-ace9-208c5cc6a263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171070} {:answer/id #uuid "10a2ca61-b1fd-543e-a25d-6398f268236d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497838} {:answer/id #uuid "16063982-ad9c-5993-87f3-955d9e76798a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497851} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcc98a2-495e-5717-bd28-655a11483875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498062} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fe28bf-bb1b-528d-8fe1-bb8dad80372e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a2ca61-b1fd-543e-a25d-6398f268236d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497838} :answer-field/id #uuid "af421b2e-a3ef-5ec4-b49e-0afb0706b4c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a58b47f-7007-5bd7-bf0e-68281bd0b768" :question/math-academy-id "q-52129" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $|-5x|-2\\cdot |-2x|.$" :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x|-2\\cdot |2x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x|-2\\cdot |2x| & = \\\\ |5|\\cdot |x|-2\\cdot |2|\\cdot |x| & = \\\\ 5|x|-2\\cdot 2\\cdot |x| & = \\\\ 5|x|-4|x| & = \\\\ |x| & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175725 :question/id #uuid "2ed3f2e8-2d0e-59e0-a9dc-1856245b768c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1343" :question/problem "Factor $3x^2 - 21x + 30.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out a $3$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-21x+30 & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-7x+10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial $x^2-7x+10$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(10),$ but only the negative factor pairs since the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$ is negative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1,-10),\\,(-2,-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-10) & =-11 & \\times  \\\\ (-2)+(-5) & =-7 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-7x=-2x-5x,$ and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7x+10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-5x+10 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(-5x+10) & = \\\\ x(x-2)-5(x-2) & = \\\\ (x-5)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n3\\left(x^2-7x+10\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n3(x-5)(x-2)\n\t}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175726 :question/id #uuid "3d3f2cef-2ad0-5967-9eb8-e327971bda8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13559" :question/problem "Find the value of $35 \\times 10$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $35 \\times 10$ means $35$ groups of $10$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n35 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n\\underbrace{10 + 10 + ⋯ + 10}_{35\\,\\text{times}} &=  \\\\\n35tens + 0ones &=  \\\\\n3hundreds + 5tens + 0ones &=  \\\\\n&= 350 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175727 :question/id #uuid "52db05e2-06c1-537f-80cd-d7a9966e69e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13560" :question/problem "What is $17 \\times 100$?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $17 \\times 100$ means $17$ groups of $100$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n17 \\cdot 100 &=  \\\\\n\\underbrace{100 + 100 + ⋯ + 100}_{17\\,\\text{times}} &=  \\\\\n17hundreds + 0tens + 0ones &=  \\\\\n1thousands + 7hundreds + 0tens + 0ones &=  \\\\\n&= 1, 700 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175728 :question/id #uuid "fd27567a-5adb-5973-b491-fcea46e60776" :question/math-academy-id "e-13562" :question/problem "Find $79 \\times 1,000$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $79 \\times 1,000$ means $79$ groups of $1,000$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n79 \\cdot 1, 000 &=  \\\\\n\\underbrace{1, 000 + 1, 000 + ⋯ + 1, 000}_{79\\,\\text{times}} &=  \\\\\n79thousands + 0hundreds + 0tens + 0ones &=  \\\\\n7ten-thousands + 9thousands + 0hundreds + 0tens + 0ones &=  \\\\\n&= 79, 000 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175733 :question/id #uuid "1be25af9-d59f-5270-b9e1-d0c5dcd731e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14143" :question/problem "Solve $\\dfrac{2}{1-2k}=-\\dfrac{4}{3k}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $k=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $k=0$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{2}{1-2k}&=-\\dfrac{4}{3k}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{2}{1-2k}&=\\dfrac{-4}{3k}\\[5pt]\n\t\t2 \\cdot 3k &= (-4) \\cdot (1-2k)\\[5pt]\t\t\n\t\t6k&=-4+8k\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\t\n\t\t6k&=-4+8k\\[5pt]\n\t\t6k-8k &=-4+8k-8k\\[5pt]\n\t\t-2k&=-4\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{-2k}{-2} &= \\dfrac{-4}{-2} \\[5pt]\n\t\tk &= 2\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $k =   2$ is different from $k=0$ and $k=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $k=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175734 :question/id #uuid "72cf8733-9727-5a9f-8028-1dee62e5715d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14146" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{2}{4x+12}=\\dfrac{2}{x+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=-3$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\dfrac{2}{4x+12} &= \\dfrac{2}{x+3} \\\t\n\t2 \\cdot (x+3) &= (4x+12) \\cdot 2 \\\n\t2x + 6 &= 8x + 24\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t2x + 6 &= 8x + 24 \\\n\t2x + 6-24 &= 8x + 24-24 \\\n\t2x-18 &= 8x \\\n\t2x-18-2x &= 8x-2x \\\n\t-18 &= 6x \\\n        \\dfrac{-18}{6} &= \\dfrac{6x}{6} \\\n        -3 &= x\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-3$ cannot be a solution, the equation has **no solutions.**"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175735 :question/id #uuid "ca9ba6cf-860f-5868-b91f-1dff84406f22" :question/math-academy-id "e-14357" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y = 4x - 1$ and passes through the point $(-8,7).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $l_1$ denote the line $y = 4x - 1,$ and let $l_2$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of $l_2$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of $l_1.$ Since $l_1$ has a slope of $4,$ the line $l_2$ must have a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-8,7).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\ny - y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}(x-(-8)) \\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}(x+8)\\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}x - 2 \\[5pt]\ny &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}x + 5\n\t}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175739 :question/id #uuid "2d5b690c-bee9-5503-8f21-285b8c42d5fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14705" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $-\\dfrac{3}{2x}=\\dfrac{2}{x+7}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ and $x=-7$  cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t-\\dfrac{3}{2x}&=\\dfrac{2}{x+7}\\[5pt]\t\n        \\dfrac{-3}{2x}&=\\dfrac{2}{x+7}\\[5pt]\t\n\t(-3) \\cdot (x+7) &=2x \\cdot (2) \\[5pt]\t\n\t-3x - 21&=  4x\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t-3x - 21&= 4x\\[5pt]\t\n\t-3x -21 + 3x&=4x + 3x\\[5pt]\t\n\t-21&=7x\\[5pt]\t\n\t\\dfrac{-21}{7}&=\\dfrac{7x}{7} \\[5pt]\t\n\t-3 &= x\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $x =   - 3$ is different from $x=0$ and $x=-7$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x= -3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175797 :question/id #uuid "1c852599-e08e-5b54-be2b-700c3f8f47f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6931" :question/problem "Factor the expression $-2x^2-22x-48.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out a $-2$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-22x-48 & = \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+11x+24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial $x^2+11x+24$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(24)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,24),\\,(2,12),\\,(3,8),\\,(4,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(11)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+24 & =25 & \\times  \\\\ 2+12 & =14 & \\times  \\\\ 3+8 & =11 & ✓ \\\\ 4+6 & =10 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $11x=3x+8x,$ and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n-2\\left(x^2+11x+24\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\left(x^2+3x+8x+24\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\left((x^2+3x)+(8x+24)\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\Bigl(x(x+3)+8(x+3)\\Bigr)&=\\[5pt]\n-2(x+8)(x+3)\n\t}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175812 :question/id #uuid "054cbbc8-22ef-585d-bf8a-849eabdc09a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8800" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to $x^3-3x^2+12x-10 = 0$ given that $x=1+3\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x) = x^3-3x^2+12x-10.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and $x_1=1+3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{1+3\\textrm{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$\\big(x-(1+3\\text{i})\\big)\\big(x-(1-3\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(1+3\\text{i}+1-3\\text{i})x+(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+({1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2 - 2x + 10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-2x+10\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+12x-10\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+2x-10 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,-\\,{x}^{2}+2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x^2 - 2x + 10)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1, 1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c8cc82-1a8f-5ca4-9560-c7203ac0c32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{4},2\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236064} {:answer/id #uuid "45b149bc-5e13-5e23-ac03-e58b885732ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{8},2\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236266} {:answer/id #uuid "73676e8c-e747-52fd-b1bf-d1b61ebc0f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{2},2-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236548} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff4d198-9592-5c8f-b3e6-effd041126b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},2\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236612} {:answer/id #uuid "8df84908-08d3-5988-9299-2a66e3ed39fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{8},2-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff4d198-9592-5c8f-b3e6-effd041126b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},2\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236612} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ac50338-18f3-5438-abd9-46607576017a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1dbcaa6d-a062-5bd6-9123-63e6ea9f493b" :question/math-academy-id "q-101758" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to $2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8=0$ given that $x=2-2\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=2-2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{2-2\\text{i}}=2+2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(2-2\\text{i}))(x-(2+2\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(2-2\\text{i}))(x-(2+2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(2-2\\text{i}+2+2\\text{i})x+(2-2\\text{i})(2+2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+({2}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+8 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-4x+8$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x-1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-4x+8\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+16x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+4x-8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,{x}^{2}+4x-8}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+8)(2x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=\\frac{1}{2},2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a52351-b0ea-5214-b447-eb8bfc25cede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174919} {:answer/id #uuid "c44caa58-b68e-5fc5-ae37-46ffc70f9415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174920} {:answer/id #uuid "e5830896-5bea-546f-8950-0461dcc18f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174921} {:answer/id #uuid "e88abefb-6bda-5f3c-9741-77dfe56290a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174922} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddace94-63fa-5071-994a-652bc14cb466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c44caa58-b68e-5fc5-ae37-46ffc70f9415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174920} :answer-field/id #uuid "e57dad43-30f5-5af5-b15c-170042285046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c398936-3982-5ba0-b353-c27acab63b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-32105" :question/problem "If $-3{a}^{2}+30a-72=-3(a+p)(a+q),$ then $p+q=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out a $-3$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}^{2}+30a-72 & =-3({a}^{2}-10a+24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${a}^{2}-10a+24$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(24).$ We will want to find the factor pair that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10),$ so we will consider the negative factor pairs in particular:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & \\times (-24) \\\\ (-2) & \\times (-12) \\\\ (-3) & \\times (-8) \\\\ (-4) & \\times (-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-24) & =-25 & \\times  \\\\ (-2)+(-12) & =-14 & \\times  \\\\ (-3)+(-8) & =-11 & \\times  \\\\ (-4)+(-6) & =-10 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $-10a=-4a-6a,$ and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({a}^{2}-10a+24) & = \\\\ -3({a}^{2}-4a-6a+24) & = \\\\ -3(a(a-4)-6(a-4)) & = \\\\ -3(a-4)(a-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-4,q=-6,$ and $p+q=-10.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179461 :question/id #uuid "27032e03-f6b6-5f66-b514-d3b9c4f79ec0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1352" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{y^4 z^{6}}$ given that $z \\lt 0.$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{y^4 z^{6}} & = \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\sqrt{y^2 z^3 \\cdot y^2 z^3} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\sqrt{\\left(y^2 z^3\\right)^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\t|y^2 z^3| & = \\[5pt]\n|y^2| \\cdot |z^3| & = \\[5pt]\n|y^2| \\cdot |z|^3\n\t}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have $|y^2| = y^2.$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that $z \\lt 0,$ and to take the absolute value of any negative number we need to flip the sign, so we have $|z| = -z.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}^{2}|\\cdot |z{|}^{3}={y}^{2}\\cdot (-z)^{3}=-{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179462 :question/id #uuid "4b7c6339-6e9c-5047-a95f-3daf41db4a8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1353" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{s^2}}$ given that $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{s^2}} &= \\[5pt]\n\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r}{s} \\cdot \\dfrac{r}{s}} &= \\[5pt]\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{r}{s}\\right)^2} &= \\[5pt]\n\n\\left|\\dfrac{r}{s}\\right| &= \\[5pt]\n\n\\dfrac{|r|}{|s|} & \n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe are told that $s \\gt 0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|s| = s.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{|r|}{|s|} = \\dfrac{|r|}{s} .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179463 :question/id #uuid "13f8fdac-32c2-5f2a-a818-cde14e59299e" :question/math-academy-id "e-1373" :question/problem "Simplify $9\\sqrt{p - 4} - 7\\sqrt{p - 4} .$" :question/worked-solution "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{p - 4}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\sqrt{p-4}-7\\sqrt{p-4} & = \\\\ (9-7)\\sqrt{p-4} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{p-4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179464 :question/id #uuid "eb35e7da-e6d0-5811-a2ee-92390f71c5f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-1374" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{12x}-\\sqrt{27x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{12x}-\\sqrt{27x} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4\\cdot 3x}-\\sqrt{9\\cdot 3x} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3x}-\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{3x} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot \\sqrt{3x}-3\\cdot \\sqrt{3x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3x}-3\\sqrt{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3x}-3\\sqrt{3x} & = \\\\ (2-3)\\cdot \\sqrt{3x} & = \\\\ (-1)\\cdot \\sqrt{3x} & = \\\\ -\\sqrt{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179475 :question/id #uuid "d1904d74-7f31-5211-b7d4-88f4f5042d64" :question/math-academy-id "e-13982" :question/problem "Find the point where the line $4x-3y=8$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x.$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t4x-3y&=8\\[5pt]\n\t4x-3(0)&=8\\[5pt]\n\t4x&=8\\[5pt]\n\t\\dfrac{4x}{4}&=\\dfrac{8}{4}\\[5pt]\n\tx&=2\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $2,$ and we conclude that the line intersects the $x$-axis at $\\left(2,0\\right).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179508 :question/id #uuid "26c11357-893b-5921-a9ea-d3db80d36de0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4621" :question/problem "Find $\\sqrt[3]{12} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{12} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Grouping up the square roots and the cube roots, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{12}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{12}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{12} & = \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{12}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{12}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{12})(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}) & = \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{12})}^{3}{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ 12\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ 36 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179509 :question/id #uuid "0346b5d4-3d16-53e5-a25d-4f770ef5f863" :question/math-academy-id "e-5127" :question/problem "Write $2 + \\dfrac{2}{k}$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that\n\n$$\n2=\\dfrac 2 1\n$$\n\nwe can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 1 + \\dfrac{2}{k}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $k$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{1} + \\frac{2}{k} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{k}{k} \\cdot \\frac{2}{1} + \\frac{2}{k} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{2k}{k} + \\frac{2}{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2k}{k} + \\frac{2}{k} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2k + 2}{k} &=  \\\\\n&= (2(k + 1))/(k)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179510 :question/id #uuid "866c1453-6d54-584c-ae65-0bc495ebf23a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5130" :question/problem "Write $\\dfrac{1}{q} + \\dfrac{1}{p+1}$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $(p+1)q$, since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $p+1$, and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{q} + \\frac{1}{p + 1} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{p + 1}{p + 1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{q} + \\frac{q}{q} \\cdot \\frac{1}{p + 1} &=  \\\\\n(p + 1)/((p + 1)q) + (q)/((p + 1)q) &=  \\\\\n&= (p + q + 1)/((p + 1)q)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179514 :question/id #uuid "6dfde17c-e23e-5137-86cb-f8b756788f13" :question/math-academy-id "e-5669" :question/problem "Given that $n \\geq 2$ is an integer, simplify $\\dfrac {n!} {(n - 2)!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\nn! = n \\cdot (n-1) \\cdot (n-2)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n-2)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {n!} {(n - 2)!} \n&= \\dfrac {n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot (n - 2)!} {(n - 2)!}\\[5pt] \n\n&= \\dfrac {n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot \\cancel{(n - 2)!}} {\\cancel{(n - 2)!}}\\[5pt] \n\n&= n(n -1) \\[5pt]\n&= n^2 - n\n\t}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179515 :question/id #uuid "357d19f9-977d-5143-a881-3ecbc068556c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5670" :question/problem "Given that $k \\geq 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\dfrac {k^2(k - 1)!} {k!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that \n\n$$\nk! = k \\cdot (k-1)!\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify the numerator as follows:\n\n$$\nk^2 (k-1)! = k \\cdot \\underbrace{k \\cdot (k-1)!}_{k!} = k \\cdot k!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $k!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {k^2(k - 1)!} {k!} \n&= \\dfrac {k \\cdot k!} {k!}\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {k \\cdot \\cancel{k!}} {\\cancel{k!}} \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {k} {1} \\[5pt]\n&= k\n\t}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179619 :question/id #uuid "213ad0ee-813b-5a4b-8926-5fbd1aed4906" :question/math-academy-id "e-9723" :question/problem "Simplify $10\\sqrt[3]{y} + 2\\sqrt[3]{y} - 7\\sqrt{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{y}$ terms together, but we cannot combine the $\\sqrt[3]{y}$ and $\\sqrt{y}$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t10\\sqrt[3]{y} + 2\\sqrt[3]{y} - 7\\sqrt{y} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t(10 + 2)\\sqrt[3]{y} - 7\\sqrt{y} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t12\\sqrt[3]{y}-7\\sqrt{y} &\n\t}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0cfe5f9-b62f-5b51-ab1d-bce042907edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178516} {:answer/id #uuid "414b8ab5-e58e-5e5f-afd8-5d24e4765f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178518} {:answer/id #uuid "5af6a186-f70b-53bc-a5e9-a363d3f26134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178520} {:answer/id #uuid "b31894e7-afeb-578b-a355-395a21451c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500841} {:answer/id #uuid "fadb7efd-f41c-5bbf-86ff-9b2b6e0d2516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0cfe5f9-b62f-5b51-ab1d-bce042907edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178516} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d10b6a6-f98d-52cb-975a-842633722f73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec502873-fbf5-5e68-afb5-0d5690849cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-2930" :question/problem "Find $\\sqrt{{a}^{2}}$ given that $a>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(a)^{2}} & = \\\\ |a| & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $a>0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|a|=a.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a|=a.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f829370c-4dbd-5a86-aadf-e393a88dd583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178626} {:answer/id #uuid "73a9386c-4b96-529c-8a82-c7219e61ceea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x\\sqrt{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178629} {:answer/id #uuid "2aab4d00-4a59-532b-ae36-4832335d301e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\sqrt{5}+x\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500550} {:answer/id #uuid "51a2289a-f9a9-5068-84bc-9c5f85953461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x\\sqrt{5}+x\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500634} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd0a3f0-00f1-520b-9c34-359a3e5fdad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sqrt{45}+x\\sqrt{x}-2x\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aab4d00-4a59-532b-ae36-4832335d301e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\sqrt{5}+x\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500550} :answer-field/id #uuid "32d1a6c3-5fc3-52c1-b24b-bcb67ac33ee0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93852038-6a90-54a4-b5b0-75d906c1e893" :question/math-academy-id "q-4060" :question/problem "$2x\\sqrt{45}+x\\sqrt{x}-2x\\sqrt{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\sqrt{45}+x\\sqrt{x}-2x\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 2x\\sqrt{9\\cdot 5}+x\\sqrt{x}-2x\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 2x\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}+x\\sqrt{x}-2x\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 2x\\cdot 3\\sqrt{5}+x\\sqrt{x}-2x\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 6x\\sqrt{5}+x\\sqrt{x}-2x\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x\\sqrt{5}+x\\sqrt{x}-2x\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ (6x-2x)\\sqrt{5}+x\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 4x\\sqrt{5}+x\\sqrt{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115182fb-23d1-507d-8d32-0688ec6d2075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4a-11}{a-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324130} {:answer/id #uuid "1576c91e-c2b6-5958-bc7e-7045e1254ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4a+11}{a-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324163} {:answer/id #uuid "413823fc-2d0d-5f08-8246-7d8fe18ead7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2a-7}{a-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324498} {:answer/id #uuid "8518319d-b371-57c4-a616-a8e0b63e92cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4a-7}{a-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325094} {:answer/id #uuid "a96c366a-3b2a-56d2-a87f-2f15f05c5144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2a-11}{a-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a96c366a-3b2a-56d2-a87f-2f15f05c5144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2a-11}{a-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325410} :answer-field/id #uuid "a77e5afa-d7d0-5647-b737-d8f5b5d8e56f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4906f3f-5058-5671-b3a5-090c5552dbcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-41566" :question/problem "Write $3-\\frac{a+2}{a-3}$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $3=\\frac{3}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $a-3$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $a-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{a-3}{a-3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(a-3)-(a+2)}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3a-9-a-2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2a-11}{a-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43113724-4737-55bd-be10-ea13f354c498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n+4}{4n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324512} {:answer/id #uuid "557c2210-cde2-5a90-ab2d-278d391fa572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n+4}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324681} {:answer/id #uuid "7c80d65a-f4b4-5976-85e0-2d42b91e04f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(n+1)}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325018} {:answer/id #uuid "86d696a3-51f6-5c74-b60a-ee777cf766f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(n+1)}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325119} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c3cf18-f2f8-5303-b271-47d390e13977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4n+1}{4n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c80d65a-f4b4-5976-85e0-2d42b91e04f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(n+1)}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325018} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3a0ac12-5ec7-59fe-bcab-532f6789e555" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65698589-f97d-56e6-8561-436b3f3f5011" :question/math-academy-id "q-41574" :question/problem "Write $4+\\frac{4}{n}$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $4=\\frac{4}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $n$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{n}{n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n+4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(n+1)}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183924 :question/id #uuid "683ba911-d266-50ed-b4b3-a3b665d1661e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10643" :question/problem "Express $\\left(2.4 \\times 10^{7}\\right) + \\left(3.7 \\times 10^{8} \\right)$ in scientific notation." :question/worked-solution "The first number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the first number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2.4 \\cdot 10^{7} &=  \\\\\n2.4 \\cdot 10^{8 - 1} &=  \\\\\n2.4 \\cdot 10^{-1 + 8} &=  \\\\\n2.4 \\cdot 10^{-1} \\cdot 10^{8} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.24 \\cdot 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the addition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2.4 \\cdot 10^{7}) + (3.7 \\cdot 10^{8}) &=  \\\\\n(0.24 \\cdot 10^{8}) + (3.7 \\cdot 10^{8}) &=  \\\\\n(0.24 + 3.7) \\cdot 10^{8} &=  \\\\\n&= 3.94 \\cdot 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183925 :question/id #uuid "04d9231e-da3d-59f6-9cb3-e6ce4e37ff76" :question/math-academy-id "e-10653" :question/problem "Express $\\left(3 \\times 10^{-4}\\right) + \\left(5 \\times 10^{-3} \\right)$ in scientific notation." :question/worked-solution "The first number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the second number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot 10^{-4} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 10^{-3 - 1} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 10^{-1 - 3} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 10^{-1} \\cdot 10^{-3} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.3 \\cdot 10^{-3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the addition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 \\cdot 10^{-4}) + (5 \\cdot 10^{-3}) &=  \\\\\n(0.3 \\cdot 10^{-3}) + (5 \\cdot 10^{-3}) &=  \\\\\n(0.3 + 5) \\cdot 10^{-3} &=  \\\\\n&= 5.3 \\cdot 10^{-3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183926 :question/id #uuid "4afa6118-a546-550a-a1e0-5b61d68770f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10682" :question/problem "Express $\\left(7 \\times 10^{10} \\right) + \\left(2\\times 10^{12} \\right)$ in scientific notation." :question/worked-solution "The first number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the second number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 \\cdot 10^{10} &=  \\\\\n7 \\cdot 10^{12 - 2} &=  \\\\\n7 \\cdot 10^{-2 + 12} &=  \\\\\n7 \\cdot 10^{-2} \\cdot 10^{12} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.07 \\cdot 10^{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the addition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(7 \\cdot 10^{10}) + (2 \\cdot 10^{12}) &=  \\\\\n(0.07 \\cdot 10^{12}) + (2 \\cdot 10^{12}) &=  \\\\\n&= (0.07 + 2) \\cdot 10^{12} \\\\\n&= 2.07 \\cdot 10^{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183927 :question/id #uuid "d29336c7-ff49-5af0-b13c-74ce1fae0c79" :question/math-academy-id "e-10683" :question/problem "Astronomers observe the motion of Mars' two moons, Phobos and Deimos, and use these observations to approximate their masses. The mass of Phobos is approximately $1.07\\times 10^{16}\\,\\textrm{kg}$, while the mass of Deimos is approximately $1.5 \\times 10^{15}\\,\\textrm{kg}$. What is the total mass of Mars' two moons?" :question/worked-solution "We need to write the following expression in scientific notation:\n$1.07 \\times 10^{16} + 1.5 \\times 10^{15}$\n\nThe second number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the first number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.5 \\cdot 10^{15} &=  \\\\\n1.5 \\cdot 10^{16 - 1} &=  \\\\\n1.5 \\cdot 10^{-1 + 16} &=  \\\\\n1.5 \\cdot 10^{-1} \\cdot 10^{16} &=  \\\\\n&= 0.15 \\cdot 10^{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the addition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1.07 \\cdot 10^{16}) + (1.5 \\cdot 10^{15}) &=  \\\\\n(1.07 \\cdot 10^{16}) + (0.15 \\cdot 10^{16}) &=  \\\\\n(1.07 + 0.15) \\cdot 10^{16} &=  \\\\\n&= 1.22 \\cdot 10^{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the two moons is $1.22 \\times 10^{16} \\,\\textrm{kg}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183929 :question/id #uuid "409f2685-2213-5c50-be32-5074b04d0df0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12865" :question/problem "Expand $(3p + 4q + 2)\\cdot 5$" :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3p + 4q + 2) \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n3p \\cdot 5 + 4q \\cdot 5 + 2 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n(15p) + (20q) + (10) &=  \\\\\n&= 15p + 20q + 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183930 :question/id #uuid "c890dec9-2830-5757-8360-07184eed5a88" :question/math-academy-id "e-12867" :question/problem "Expand $4(5+4a-2b)$" :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(5 + 4a - 2b) &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot 5 + 4 \\cdot (4a) + 4 \\cdot (-2b) &=  \\\\\n(20) + (16a) + (-8b) &=  \\\\\n20 + 16a - 8b &=  \\\\\n&= 16a - 8b + 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183931 :question/id #uuid "3823b471-c32a-54db-84b5-cd85e9049bf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12868" :question/problem "Expand $(3x - 5y + 1) \\cdot (-4)$." :question/worked-solution "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $(-4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3x - 5y + 1) \\cdot (-4) &=  \\\\\n3x \\cdot (-4) + (-5y) \\cdot (-4) + 1 \\cdot (-4) &=  \\\\\n(-12x) + (20y) + (-4) &=  \\\\\n&= - 12x + 20y - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183932 :question/id #uuid "cd1c71d8-a25d-5242-8f80-7e3e149fbc19" :question/math-academy-id "e-13397" :question/problem "By rounding the dividend to the nearest ten, estimate the quotient of $448\\div 5$." :question/worked-solution "We round the dividend to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n448\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 5 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 450 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}5\n$$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $450 \\div 5$.\n\nRemember that $450$ is the same as\n\n$$\n450 = 45 \\times 10\n$$\n\nSo, we can solve this division problem using place value strategies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n450 \\div 5 &=  \\\\\n45 \\cdot 10 \\div 5 &=  \\\\\n45 \\div 5 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n(45 \\div 5) \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n9 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 90\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n448 \\div 5 \\approx 90\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183934 :question/id #uuid "6d3502a1-7578-551f-b8e9-fc791587eea3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13400" :question/problem "A farmer needs to pack $27,825\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of potatoes into bags. Each bag fits exactly $7\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of potatoes. By rounding the total weight of potatoes to the nearest thousand kilograms, estimate the number of bags needed to pack all the potatoes." :question/worked-solution "To find the number of bags needed to pack the potatoes, we need to divide $27,825$ by $7$.\n\nFirst, we round the dividend to the nearest thousand:\n\n$$\n27, 825\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 7 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 28, 000 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}7\n$$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $28,000 \\div 7$.\n\nRemember that $28,000$ is the same as\n\n$$\n28,000 = 28 \\times 1,000\n$$\n\nSo, we can solve this division problem using place value strategies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n28, 000 \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n28 \\cdot 1, 000 \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n28 \\div 7 \\cdot 1, 000 &=  \\\\\n(28 \\div 7) \\cdot 1, 000 &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot 1, 000 &=  \\\\\n&= 4, 000\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n27,825 \\div 7 \\approx 4,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the farmer needs about $4,000$ bags."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183935 :question/id #uuid "d669aafc-c1f1-5acd-b7d8-5ff0f31178b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1361" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3} &= \\\n\t\t\\sqrt{x \\cdot x^3} &= \\\n\t\t\\sqrt{x^4} &= \\\n\t\t|x^2|\n\t}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{x}^{2}| & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183936 :question/id #uuid "d6b143d1-7d3f-5836-8797-19cb37181541" :question/math-academy-id "e-1364" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{16x^2}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{16{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{4x} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{16{x}^{2}\\cdot 4x} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{64{x}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(4x)^{3}} & = \\\\ 4x & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183946 :question/id #uuid "ab4772a2-f980-5ad7-a377-34e34b95be83" :question/math-academy-id "e-14403" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\,{3^{1-x^4}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u= 1-x^4.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\textrm{d}u}{ \\textrm{d}x} = -4x^3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}x = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int {x^3\\,{3^{1-x^4}} }\\, \\textrm{d}x \n&=\\int {{3^{1-x^4}}\\cdot x^3 }\\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n&=  \\int {3^{u}} \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n                                                     &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\int {3^{u}}  \\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=  -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\cdot\\dfrac{3^{u}}{\\ln 3} + C\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=   -\\dfrac{3^{1-x^4}}{4\\ln 3}+ C.\n}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184131 :question/id #uuid "cd3fba6a-bb16-51b1-b2ea-fb88656d5aa6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9979" :question/problem "Find an expression that's equal to $\\sqrt{5w^3} \\cdot \\sqrt{uv}$ by combining the radicals." :question/worked-solution "We apply the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5{w}^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{uv} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{5{w}^{3}\\cdot uv} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{5uv{w}^{3}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184132 :question/id #uuid "06df772e-eb43-55e8-9ede-3fc2f7ae291d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9984" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{7}{25})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right)\n \t\t\t\t -  \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left( \\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {72} {125} -  \\dfrac {28} {125} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {100} {125}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {4} {5}.\n\t}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188083 :question/id #uuid "528d580f-a39c-5374-a08f-49a3373f2256" :question/math-academy-id "e-12888" :question/problem "What is $36\\div 6 \\times 10$?" :question/worked-solution "When the only operations are multiplication and division, we carry out the operations from left to right, using parentheses $(\\cdots)$ to make our working clearer:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n36 \\div 6 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n(36 \\div 6) \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n6 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188084 :question/id #uuid "d3d4b515-dec0-5cba-8ca5-524fe1ec0ad7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12889" :question/problem "Compute $2\\div 8 \\times 12$." :question/worked-solution "When the only operations are multiplication and division,\n\n- we carry out the operations from left to right, using parentheses $(\\cdots)$ to make our working clearer, and\n- we can swap the order in which the operations are carried out.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\div 8 \\cdot 12 &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 12 \\div 8 &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 12) \\div 8 &=  \\\\\n24 \\div 8 &=  \\\\\n&= 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188085 :question/id #uuid "6299c6bd-1577-5c57-8782-0a31bf1ce877" :question/math-academy-id "e-12890" :question/problem "Find $24\\times 5 \\div 3$." :question/worked-solution "When the only operations are multiplication and division,\n\n- we carry out the operations from left to right, using parentheses $(\\cdots)$ to make our working clearer, and\n- we can swap the order in which the operations are carried out.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24 \\cdot 5 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n24 \\div 3 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n(24 \\div 3) \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 40 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188086 :question/id #uuid "7f43385c-ccc0-5855-916a-e6bb689c290f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13094" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $-3-(5+2u).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-3 - 5 - 2u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3-5-2u & = \\\\ (-3-5)-2u & = \\\\ -8-2u & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188087 :question/id #uuid "d73b6911-278d-5c8e-a900-fc751fa518fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-13095" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $11-(5a-7).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n11-5a + 7.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11-5a+7 & = \\\\ (11+7)-5a & = \\\\ 18-5a & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188088 :question/id #uuid "34317825-e142-5f97-951f-45f87d9672d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13096" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $-9v-4(3+5v).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $-9v$ must be expanded using the distributive law. \n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n-9v - \\Big[4(3+5v)\\Big]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n-9v - \\Big[12+20v\\Big].\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-9v - 12 - 20v.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9v-12-20v & = \\\\ (-9v-20v)-12 & = \\\\ -29v-12 & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188089 :question/id #uuid "d6283f26-9e20-5255-b358-8bc0c6523065" :question/math-academy-id "e-13097" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $10 - 2(3x - 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $10$ must be expanded using the distributive law. \n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n10 - \\Big[2(3x-4)\\Big]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n10 - \\Big[6x-8\\Big].\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n10 - 6x + 8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-6x+8 & = \\\\ (10+8)-6x & = \\\\ 18-6x & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188147 :question/id #uuid "3d55ec05-3194-5453-932c-b7fee736d659" :question/math-academy-id "e-13544" :question/problem "Sarah will use $90$ beads to make $3$ identical necklaces. How many beads will each necklace have?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the number of beads each necklace will have, we need to calculate $90\\div 3$.\n\nRemember that $90$ in expanded form is\n\n$$\n9 \\times 10\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n90 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 10 \\div 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap the order of the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 \\cdot 10 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n9 \\div 3 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n(9 \\div 3) \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, each necklace will have $30$ beads."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188148 :question/id #uuid "715fe8f0-311e-5efe-8835-0059eb43ef3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13545" :question/problem "What is $280 \\div 7$?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $280$ is the same as $28$ tens. So, we have\n\n$$\n280 = 28 \\times {10}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n280 \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 28 \\cdot 10 \\div 7 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap the order of the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n28 \\cdot 10 \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n28 \\div 7 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n(28 \\div 7) \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n280\\div 7 = 40\n$$"}]
